From 49afbb608b801589c89fe2f882f7e36c35cb433e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 14:44:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] Refresh wallet grants between slow host passes

A published wallet allow lives only as long as its decision (at most 90 s
past its chain snapshot), but the host pass that publishes it walks every
tracked deployment serially and, under public-RPC rate limits, takes
minutes. On the minipc the adapter was admitted about 73 s of every
7 minutes while its lease, proof and channel were fresh.

Each allow this process published is now re-decided, independently of
the pass, once fewer than 40 s remain:
- per deployment, one refresh in flight, each bounded (25 s), at most 4
  at once, earliest deadline first, so one slow decision never starves
  another deployment;
- from a fresh ledger row: refreshed only while it is still active, ours,
  leased and served here with the same owner, visibility, version and
  config the pass granted. The host wallet decision is asked anew for
  exactly that row (forPrivate from the row), and only its own deadline
  is published;
- reads only. A refusal withdraws the allow at once. An unknown or a
  timeout publishes nothing, so the allow lapses at its own deadline;
- fenced: every revoke and hold removes the grant before writing the
  channel, and a result is dropped if the grant, the channel or the
  tracking changed meanwhile;
- only deployments the network agent routes are re-decided (its status
  narrows the work; it is never an authority).

(cherry picked from commit ad4a72a83de85db72a5c5ddce45a5c8d8599140e)
